      "x": -593.3083119198454,
      "y": -854.8682792602565,
      "width": 760.8394165039062,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "KNS5n0fWJIHhRf6mcOM8j",
      "index": "a0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 304724920,
      "version": 282,
      "versionNonce": 1996968136,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784681837339,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This chapter covers\nWhy standard attention becomes memory-bound and how to spot that shift.\nCore ideas behind Flash Attention: tiling, online softmax, and SRAM residency.\nRestructuring loops and work distribution so tiles cooperate efficiently.\nBuilding a fused Flash Attention kernel with WMMA tensor cores.\nEvaluating Flash Attention across versions and hardware generations.\nDeciding when Flash Attention is the right optimization for your model.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This chapter covers\nWhy standard attention becomes memory-bound and how to spot that shift.\nCore ideas behind Flash Attention: tiling, online softmax, and SRAM residency.\nRestructuring loops and work distribution so tiles cooperate efficiently.\nBuilding a fused Flash Attention kernel with WMMA tensor cores.\nEvaluating Flash Attention across versions and hardware generations.\nDeciding when Flash Attention is the right optimization for your model.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "AaozTe_1NmAMqtI9hV_Wb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -604.4124507343604,
      "y": -662.9939025758083,
      "width": 1626.6788330078125,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "KNS5n0fWJIHhRf6mcOM8j",
      "index": "a1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 233407672,
      "version": 643,
      "versionNonce": 980547528,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784681837339,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "8.1.1 from compute-bound to memory-bound\n    Tensor core: raw compute throughput\n    Memory bottlenecks attention\n\nResolving compute-bound: Tesnor Cores (specialized hardware)\nOptimizing attention mechnisms encounter differnt kind of performance barrier (memory bound)\n\nBaseline naive/standard self-attention mechanism relies on the high-bandwidth memory (HBM), moving such large compute in/out outweighs the actual cost of compute\nWe are no longer limited by how fast we can do the math, but by how fast we can shuttle data to and from the GPU’s main memory.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "8.1.1 from compute-bound to memory-bound\n    Tensor core: raw compute throughput\n    Memory bottlenecks attention\n\nResolving compute-bound: Tesnor Cores (specialized hardware)\nOptimizing attention mechnisms encounter differnt kind of performance barrier (memory bound)\n\nBaseline naive/standard self-attention mechanism relies on the high-bandwidth memory (HBM), moving such large compute in/out outweighs the actual cost of compute\nWe are no longer limited by how fast we can do the math, but by how fast we can shuttle data to and from the GPU’s main memory.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "dwLbX7JP0MQi2lqY9rxDL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 288.5683878242817,
      "y": -842.715796044851,
      "width": 949.599365234375,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "KNS5n0fWJIHhRf6mcOM8j",
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1892167096,
      "version": 244,
      "versionNonce": 670023368,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784681837339,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Chp 8 Takeaways\n-> Tensor Core gives raw compute throughput\n-> Attention has a different bottleneck: volume of data between on-chip SRAM and off-chip HBM\n    -> Flash Attention",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Chp 8 Takeaways\n-> Tensor Core gives raw compute throughput\n-> Attention has a different bottleneck: volume of data between on-chip SRAM and off-chip HBM\n    -> Flash Attention",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wsB632BbuxSXfd1sjgtUT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -575.5721213552042,
      "y": -378.5132076955076,
      "width": 1671.018798828125,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "KNS5n0fWJIHhRf6mcOM8j",
      "index": "a3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 870954680,
      "version": 1166,
      "versionNonce": 1344054728,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784681837339,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "8.1.2 The High Bandwidth memory bottleneck in standard attention\nTo understand why Flash Attention is necessary, first analyze the standard implementation of the attention mechanism\n\nThe core of attention\n    1. calculate the dot product of Query (Q) and Key (K) matrices, Score (S) = Q @ (K) dot (T)\n    2. apply softmax to the score to get weights (P), P = softmax(S)\n    3. multiply the attention weights P by the value (V) matrix to get output, O = P @ V\n\nFor a context length, N = 8192, (N, d) (S, P) = (N, N) -> 8192 x 8192 x 4 bytes (32-bit floats) ~~ 268 megabytes\n... for a modern context length N = 128k ... >65GB for a single attention head\n    This is the throughput problem transferring between memory, not the computational throughput\n** the entire S matrix must be written to HBM after the first matrix multiplication, read back from HBM, writes back to HBM, reads again for final matrix multiplication **\n-> fast onchip SRAM and slower offchip HBM is the bottleneck\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "8.1.2 The High Bandwidth memory bottleneck in standard attention\nTo understand why Flash Attention is necessary, first analyze the standard implementation of the attention mechanism\n\nThe core of attention\n    1. calculate the dot product of Query (Q) and Key (K) matrices, Score (S) = Q @ (K) dot (T)\n    2. apply softmax to the score to get weights (P), P = softmax(S)\n    3. multiply the attention weights P by the value (V) matrix to get output, O = P @ V\n\nFor a context length, N = 8192, (N, d) (S, P) = (N, N) -> 8192 x 8192 x 4 bytes (32-bit floats) ~~ 268 megabytes\n... for a modern context length N = 128k ... >65GB for a single attention head\n    This is the throughput problem transferring between memory, not the computational throughput\n** the entire S matrix must be written to HBM after the first matrix multiplication, read back from HBM, writes back to HBM, reads again for final matrix multiplication **\n-> fast onchip SRAM and slower offchip HBM is the bottleneck\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
